1. The Root Route (/)
URL: http://localhost:8080/
What to expect: This is the entry point.
It should display: "Welcome to Harshitha's Secure App!"
Why check this: It confirms that your basic @RestController is loaded and talking to the browser.

2. The Hello Route (/hello)
URL: http://localhost:8080/hello
What to expect: It should display: "Hello World! You have successfully logged in via PostgreSQL."
Why check this: This confirms that your request successfully passed through the entire "Security Handshake."
Spring Security intercepted the request.
MyUserDetailsService queried your PostgreSQL database.
UserPrincipal wrapped the data correctly.
The NoOpPasswordEncoder matched your password.

3. The "Negative" Test (Very Important!)
To be a good developer, you must also check if the "Security Guard" is actually blocking people.
Open an Incognito/Private window in your browser.
Go to http://localhost:8080/hello.
When the box pops up, type a wrong password (like h999).
Expectation: It should keep asking for the password or show an "Unauthorized" (401) error.
It should not let you see the message.

4. Check the IntelliJ Console
While you are clicking these links, keep an eye on the Terminal/Console tab at the bottom of IntelliJ.
If you type a username that isn't in pgAdmin, you should see your custom print statement: "User not found in Database!"
This is proof that your Java code is actively talking to your database in real-time.

Summary Table of your App
Route: /
Access: Logged in users
Logic: Standard Controller check

Route: /hello
Access: Logged in users
Logic: Full Security + DB Handshake check

Route: Any other URL
Access: Restricted
Logic: Spring Security default (404/401)

Now that you have the basic login working, you've mastered the hardest part of Spring Security: Database Integration.
The next logical step (and what Navin does in the next video) is Password Encoding.
Your database doesn't store "h123" in plain text where everyone can see it!
Does everything look correct when you hit those two URLs?